 "text": "Hi, how's everything with you? That's common issue. It' usually due to grime building up on the chain and chainrings. Sadly no, cooking oil is way too thick to handle the wear level on a bike chain. You need something thin and lubricated, like a bike chain lube. Shoot the chain off the bike and get it hanging down from something. Then push it through the loop on a clean rag. Rub the lubricant in well with a clean brush. Stickers usually come off easily, so a quick wipe with a clean cloth. Not necessarily. A dry chain that's not moving fast makes very little noise. It' only noisy when you pushing hard.",
